{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '285,300p;975,1005p' /home/gon7187/Projects/Omarchy-LenovoYogaBook9/README.md; cat /home/gon7187/Projects/Omarchy-LenovoYogaBook9/config/wireplumber/52-yoga-speakers-keep-open.conf /home/gon7187/Projects/Omarchy-LenovoYogaBook9/config/udev/90-yoga-tas2781-no-runtime-pm.rules /home/gon7187/Projects/Omarchy-LenovoYogaBook9/bin/yoga-amp-calib /home/gon7187/Projects/Omarchy-LenovoYogaBook9/bin/yoga-volume\"","aggregated_output":"Node 0x02 [Audio Output]    Converter: stream=1, channel=0   <- woofers\nNode 0x03 [Audio Output]    Converter: stream=1, channel=0   <- tweeters\n```\n\nThe woofers already receive full-range stereo. What remains of the thin sound is tone, not routing: use [`62-yoga-dolby-eq.conf`](config/pipewire/62-yoga-dolby-eq.conf) with `yoga-volume` (below), and do not install `51-yoga-bass-speakers.conf`. The file stays for machines on the legacy `snd_hda_intel` driver.\n\nA second suspect is the amplifier calibration: `tas2781_apply_calib: V1 CRC error`. The `CALI_DATA` UEFI variable holds valid-looking data for two amps, but its timestamp and CRC fields are zero, so the kernel discards it and the amps run uncalibrated.\n\n[`bin/yoga-amp-calib`](bin/yoga-amp-calib) `check` shows the data; `fix` backs the variable up to `~/.local/state/yoga-book/CALI_DATA.orig` and writes only the CRC field (crc32 of the first 84 bytes, the V1 check in `tas2781_hda.c`); `restore` puts the original back. It needs a reboot.\n\n**Calibration is lost again on the first PCM close/open.** `tas2781_hda_playback_hook` runs the amp's shutdown block on `HDA_GEN_PCM_ACT_CLOSE` and the power-up block on `OPEN`, but `tasdevice_select_tuningprm_cfg` only rewrites the calibration when the DSP configuration number changes (\"Unneeded loading dsp conf\" otherwise). WirePlumber suspends an idle sink after 5 s, which closes the PCM, so restarting the browser was enough to lose the bass until the next boot. Runtime PM of the amps (`config/udev/90-yoga-tas2781-no-runtime-pm.rules`) is not the trigger; the PCM close is. [`config/wireplumber/52-yoga-speakers-keep-open.conf`](config/wireplumber/52-yoga-speakers-keep-open.conf) sets `session.suspend-timeout-seconds = 0` on the speaker sink so the PCM stays open and the cycle never happens, at some battery cost (codec and DSP stay powered). Toggling `Speaker Config Id` (`amixer -c0 cset numid=5 1; amixer -c0 cset numid=5 0`) forces a configuration reload and re-applies the calibration without a reboot.\n\n**This was the missing bass.** With the EQ confirmed working digitally (+13 dB at 100 Hz measured on the sink monitor) there was still no low end by ear, with or without processing, and no amp profile changed that. After `yoga-amp-calib fix` and a reboot the `V1 CRC error` is gone and the bass is back. The uncalibrated amps were evidently running a conservative protection model that cut the low end.\n\n### Volume-dependent speaker imbalance (SOF)\n\nloading topology: intel/sof-tplg/sof-hda-generic-2ch.tplg\nsnd_hda_codec_alc269 ehdaudio0D0: autoconfig for ALC287: line_outs=2 type:speaker\nsnd_hda_codec_alc269 ehdaudio0D0:    speaker_outs=0\n```\n\n### Sensors work; auto-rotation still unwired\n\n`iio-sensor-proxy` is not installed by default. Once installed, everything reports correctly:\n\n```\n=== Has accelerometer (orientation: normal, tilt: vertical)\n=== Has ambient light sensor (value: 95.109005, unit: lux)\n```\n\n| Sensor | Notes |\n|---|---|\n| `als` | Working — drives auto-brightness, see below |\n| `accel_3d` | Present, reports orientation |\n| `gyro_3d` (x2) | Present |\n| `hinge` | Exposes **three** angles: `in_angl0_raw` (hinge), `in_angl1_raw` (screen), `in_angl2_raw` (keyboard) |\n\n**Auto-brightness is done** — see below. **Auto-rotation is not**, and needs care: the accelerometer reports orientation `normal` while `eDP-1` carries `transform = 2` because the panel is mounted 180° out. Rotation logic that ignores that offset will land 180° wrong. The three-angle hinge sensor is the obvious signal for switching display modes on a machine that folds.\n\n### Battery charge limiting — available, but not where you would look\n\nACPI cannot resolve the embedded-controller charge object, and there are **no** `charge_control_*` attributes under `/sys/class/power_supply/BAT*/`:\n\n```\nACPI Error: AE_NOT_FOUND, While resolving a named reference package element - \\_SB_.PC00.LPCB.H_EC.CHRG\nACPI Error: AE_NOT_FOUND, While resolving a named reference package element - \\_SB_.PC00.LPCB.H_EC.SEN3\n```\n# Lenovo Yoga Book 9 13IRU8: never close the speaker PCM.\n#\n# The TAS2781 amp driver runs a shutdown block when the HDA PCM closes and a\n# power-up block when it opens again, but the speaker calibration is only\n# written when the DSP *configuration* changes (tasdevice_select_tuningprm_cfg,\n# \"Unneeded loading dsp conf\"). So after the first close/open cycle -- e.g.\n# restarting the browser -- the amps run uncalibrated and the bass is gone\n# until the next boot. The codec also runtime-suspends on close.\n#\n# WirePlumber suspends an idle sink after 5 s, which closes the PCM. Keeping\n# the speaker sink from suspending keeps the PCM open, so the close/open\n# cycle never happens. Costs some battery (codec and DSP stay powered).\nmonitor.alsa.rules = [\n  {\n    matches = [\n      { node.name = \"alsa_output.pci-0000_00_1f.3-platform-skl_hda_dsp_generic.HiFi__Speaker__sink\" }\n    ]\n    actions = {\n      update-props = {\n        session.suspend-timeout-seconds = 0\n      }\n    }\n  }\n]\n# Keep the TAS2781 speaker amps out of runtime suspend.\n#\n# The driver autosuspends them 3 s after playback stops. Runtime resume only\n# calls tasdevice_prmg_load(cur_prog), which is a no-op when the program number\n# is unchanged, so if the amps lost their register state while suspended the\n# DSP program and speaker calibration are never written back: sound is good\n# after boot and thin again after the first pause. System resume does reset\n# and reload everything; runtime resume does not.\nACTION==\"add|bind\", SUBSYSTEM==\"i2c\", KERNEL==\"i2c-TIAS2781:00\", ATTR{power/control}=\"on\"\n#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"Make the kernel accept the TAS2781 speaker-amp calibration.\n\nThe factory calibration lives in the UEFI variable CALI_DATA. On this machine\nit holds two amps' data in the V1 layout (4 x 20-byte slots, timestamp, CRC),\nbut the timestamp and CRC fields are zero, so the kernel logs\n\"tas2781_apply_calib: V1 CRC error\", discards it and the amps run\nuncalibrated. This writes the CRC the kernel expects (crc32 of the first 84\nbytes) into the CRC field and changes nothing else.\n\n  yoga-amp-calib check     show the data and whether the CRC matches\n  yoga-amp-calib fix       back up the variable, then write the CRC (root)\n  yoga-amp-calib restore   write the backup back (root)\n\nTakes effect on the next boot: the driver reads calibration only at probe.\n\"\"\"\nimport os\nimport struct\nimport subprocess\nimport sys\nimport zlib\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\nVAR = Path('/sys/firmware/efi/efivars/CALI_DATA-1f52d2a1-bb3a-457d-bc09-43a3f4310a92')\nBACKUP = Path.home()/'.local/state/yoga-book/CALI_DATA.orig'\n\n\ndef read():\n    raw = VAR.read_bytes()\n    return raw[:4], raw[4:]\n\n\ndef expected_crc(data):\n    return zlib.crc32(data[:84]) & 0xffffffff\n\n\ndef check():\n    attrs, data = read()\n    words = struct.unpack('<22I', data[:88])\n    for dev in range(4):\n        print(f'amp {dev}:', ' '.join(f'{w:08x}' for w in words[dev*5:dev*5+5]))\n    stored, want = words[21], expected_crc(data)\n    print(f'timestamp {words[20]:08x}  crc stored {stored:08x}  expected {want:08x}')\n    print('OK: kernel will apply calibration' if stored == want else 'MISMATCH: kernel discards calibration')\n    return stored == want\n\n\ndef root_write(payload):\n    # efivarfs marks variables immutable; lift it only for this write.\n    script = ('import sys,subprocess;p=sys.argv[1];subprocess.run([\"chattr\",\"-i\",p],check=True)\\n'\n              'f=open(p,\"wb\");f.write(sys.stdin.buffer.read());f.close()\\n'\n              'subprocess.run([\"chattr\",\"+i\",p],check=False)')\n    elevate = ['sudo'] if sys.stdin.isatty() else ['pkexec']\n    subprocess.run([*elevate, 'python3', '-c', script, str(VAR)], input=payload, check=True)\n\n\ndef fix():\n    if check():\n        return\n    attrs, data = read()\n    words = struct.unpack('<22I', data[:88])\n    if not any(words[:5]) or words[0] == 2781:\n        sys.exit('Unexpected layout (empty or V2+); refusing to write')\n    if not BACKUP.exists():\n        BACKUP.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)\n        BACKUP.write_bytes(attrs+data)\n        print('Backup:', BACKUP)\n    patched = data[:84]+struct.pack('<I', expected_crc(data))+data[88:]\n    root_write(attrs+patched)\n    check()\n    print('Reboot to apply.')\n\n\ndef restore():\n    if not BACKUP.exists():\n        sys.exit('No backup at '+str(BACKUP))\n    root_write(BACKUP.read_bytes())\n    check()\n\n\nif __name__ == '__main__':\n    action = sys.argv[1] if len(sys.argv) > 1 else 'check'\n    {'check': check, 'fix': fix, 'restore': restore}.get(action, lambda: sys.exit(__doc__))()\n#!/bin/bash\n\n# Volume control that can see past a DSP sink built on PipeWire links.\n#\n# Omarchy deliberately reaches through DSP sinks to the physical one, so the\n# keys move real loudness and the processing always sees full-scale input.\n# omarchy-audio-output-sink does that by finding a PulseAudio *sink-input*\n# whose node.name starts with the DSP sink's name, plus a hardcoded case for\n# EasyEffects. JamesDSP has no such sink-input -- it feeds the hardware sink\n# over raw PipeWire links, invisible to `pactl list sink-inputs` -- so the\n# resolver returns jamesdsp_sink itself and the keys adjust a volume JamesDSP\n# ignores. The OSD moves and nothing is heard.\n#\n# This resolves through PipeWire links instead, and falls back to Omarchy's\n# resolver so behaviour is never worse than the stock path.\n\nset -uo pipefail\n\nOSD_ICON_VOLUME=volume\nOSD_ICON_MUTED=volume-muted\n\n# The ALSA sink that a DSP sink ultimately feeds.\n#\n# Not a single hop. JamesDSP's chain is:\n#   jamesdsp_sink:monitor_FL  ->  jdsp_@PwJamesDspPlugin_JamesDsp:input_FL\n#   jdsp_@...:output_FL       ->  alsa_output...Speaker__sink:playback_FL\n# so the sink apps write to never links directly to hardware. Walk the graph\n# outward instead, which also covers EasyEffects and plain filter-chains\n# without knowing anything about them.\nresolve_via_pipewire() {\n  pw-link -l 2>/dev/null | python3 -c '\nimport sys\n\nedges, cur = {}, None\nfor line in sys.stdin:\n    if not line[:1].isspace():\n        cur = line.strip().split(\":\")[0]\n    elif line.strip().startswith(\"|->\") and cur:\n        tgt = line.strip()[3:].strip().split(\":\")[0]\n        edges.setdefault(cur, set()).add(tgt)\n\nstart = sys.argv[1]\nseen, frontier = {start}, [start]\nfor _ in range(6):                       # bounded: never loop on a cycle\n    nxt = []\n    for node in frontier:\n        for peer in edges.get(node, ()):\n            if peer.startswith(\"alsa_output.\"):\n                print(peer)\n                raise SystemExit(0)\n            if peer not in seen:\n                seen.add(peer)\n                nxt.append(peer)\n    frontier = nxt\n' \"$1\"\n}\n\nphysical_sink() {\n  local sink\n  sink=\"$(pactl get-default-sink 2>/dev/null)\"\n\n  # Already physical: nothing to resolve.\n  if [[ -z $sink || $sink == alsa_output.* ]]; then\n    printf '%s\\n' \"$sink\"\n    return\n  fi\n\n  local resolved\n  resolved=\"$(resolve_via_pipewire \"$sink\")\"\n  if [[ -n $resolved ]] && pactl list sinks short 2>/dev/null | grep -q \"[[:space:]]$resolved[[:space:]]\"; then\n    printf '%s\\n' \"$resolved\"\n    return\n  fi\n\n  # Fall back to Omarchy's own resolver rather than doing something worse.\n  omarchy-audio-output-sink 2>/dev/null || printf '%s\\n' \"$sink\"\n}\n\nvolume_percent() {\n  pactl get-sink-volume \"$1\" 2>/dev/null |\n    awk 'NR == 1 { for (i = 1; i <= NF; i++) if ($i ~ /%$/) { sub(\"%\", \"\", $i); print $i; exit } }'\n}\n\nmuted() { [[ $(pactl get-sink-mute \"$1\" 2>/dev/null) == *yes ]]; }\n\nshow_osd() {\n  local sink=\"$1\" pct icon\n  pct=\"$(volume_percent \"$sink\")\"\n  if muted \"$sink\"; then icon=\"$OSD_ICON_MUTED\"; else icon=\"$OSD_ICON_VOLUME\"; fi\n  command -v omarchy-osd >/dev/null && omarchy-osd -i \"$icon\" -p \"${pct:-0}\" >/dev/null 2>&1\n}\n\naction=\"${1:-}\"\n[[ -n $action ]] || { echo \"usage: ${0##*/} <raise|lower|mute-toggle|+N|-N>\" >&2; exit 1; }\n\nsink=\"$(physical_sink)\"\n[[ -n $sink ]] || { echo \"could not resolve an audio sink to control\" >&2; exit 1; }\n\ncase \"$action\" in\nraise) action=\"+5\" ;;\nlower) action=\"-5\" ;;\nesac\n\nif [[ $action == \"mute-toggle\" ]]; then\n  # Same 250ms debounce as Omarchy: key repeat otherwise toggles twice.\n  debounce=\"${XDG_RUNTIME_DIR:-/tmp}/yoga-volume-mute.last\"\n  now=$(date +%s%3N)\n  last=0\n  [[ -r $debounce ]] && read -r last <\"$debounce\" || true\n  ((now - last < 250)) && exit 0\n  printf '%s\\n' \"$now\" >\"$debounce\"\n  pactl set-sink-mute \"$sink\" toggle\nelif [[ $action =~ ^([+-])([0-9]+)$ ]]; then\n  dir=\"${BASH_REMATCH[1]}\"\n  step=\"${BASH_REMATCH[2]}\"\n  cur=\"$(volume_percent \"$sink\")\"\n  [[ -n $cur ]] || { echo \"could not read volume for $sink\" >&2; exit 1; }\n  if [[ $dir == \"+\" ]]; then target=$((cur + step)); else target=$((cur - step)); fi\n  ((target < 0)) && target=0\n  ((target > 100)) && target=100\n  pactl set-sink-mute \"$sink\" 0\n  pactl set-sink-volume \"$sink\" \"${target}%\"\nelse\n  echo \"usage: ${0##*/} <raise|lower|mute-toggle|+N|-N>\" >&2\n  exit 1\nfi\n\n\n# --- loudness compensation -------------------------------------------------\n#\n# Ears lose bass sensitivity far faster than midrange as level drops, so a\n# fixed bass shelf sounds right loud and hollow quiet. Dolby handles this on\n# Windows with \"sliding-bass\": up to 18.6 dB of gain below 300 Hz, applied more\n# as the level falls (sliding-bass-max-gain / -xo-frequency in the tuning XML).\n#\n# PipeWire has no dynamic equivalent, but this script already owns the volume\n# path, so the shelf is set here instead -- more bass as the volume goes down.\n#\n# The filter-chain control can only be set while its node is alive; on a\n# SUSPENDED node pw-cli accepts the call and silently does nothing.\napply_loudness() {\n  local pct=\"$1\" id gain\n  id=\"$(pw-dump 2>/dev/null | jq -r '.[]|select(.info.props.\"node.name\"?==\"yoga_dolby\")|.id' | head -1)\"\n  [[ -n $id ]] || return 0\n\n  # +4 dB at full volume rising to +12 dB at silence, linear in displayed\n  # percent. Capped well below Dolby's 18.6 dB: with only the -6 dB input\n  # trim as headroom, more than +12 dB clips bass-heavy material.\n  gain=\"$(awk -v p=\"$pct\" 'BEGIN { g = 4 + 8 * (1 - p/100); if (g < 4) g = 4; if (g > 12) g = 12; printf \"%.1f\", g }')\"\n\n  pw-cli s \"$id\" Props \"{ params = [ \\\"bassL:Gain\\\" $gain \\\"bassR:Gain\\\" $gain ] }\" >/dev/null 2>&1\n}\n\napply_loudness \"$(volume_percent \"$sink\")\"\nshow_osd \"$sink\"\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
